{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"condition-check-in-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Condition check-in track","description":"Runs a regular self-management check-in for a long-term condition, from reviewing the last few weeks to small routine changes within the care plan and questions for the next appointment.","category":"chronic-conditions","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["review","plan"],"role":["individual","parent"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["notes","text"],"output":["summary","plan","questions"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["medical"],"invocation":"user","effort":"standard","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"frontier","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"basic","tags":["self-management","monthly-review","action-plan","symptom-diary","care-plan","fluctuating-conditions"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["explore-flare-triggers","plan-activity-pacing","prepare-doctor-questions"],"personas":["chronic-condition-coach"]},"args":[{"name":"condition","description":"Your condition and the self-management plan or goals your care team has given you, in their words.","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"recent","description":"Notes from the last few weeks, in any form (symptom diary, readings, what went well, what was hard, flares, appointments, changes made by your care team). Remove names and ID numbers.","type":"text","required":true}],"steps":[{"id":"review","file":"steps/01-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve"},{"id":"adjust","file":"steps/02-adjust.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"prepare","file":"steps/03-prepare.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"A regular check-in, monthly or after a rough patch, that helps someone living with a long-term condition look back honestly, make one or two small changes to how they manage day to day, and leave with clear questions for their care team. Each step produces a short document and stops.\n\n<condition>\n{{condition}}\n</condition>\n\n<recent>\n{{recent}}\n</recent>\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- Check for emergency signs and for anything the person's own plan says needs a call to the care team (for example readings outside the range they were given, a flare beyond their usual pattern, new symptoms). If present, say so first: emergency services now for emergencies, the care team today for the rest.\n- Never suggest starting, stopping or changing any medicine, dose or treatment, or interpret readings as meaning treatment should change. Those become questions for the care team.\n- Use the person's own words and numbers; mark gaps as [not noted].\n- Keep changes small and within the care team's plan: routines, pacing, tracking, practical support, and coping.\n- If the person sounds low or overwhelmed, acknowledge it, and if there is any sign of risk to their safety, point to crisis support before continuing.","steps":[{"id":"review","file":"steps/01-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Look back\n\nReview the last few weeks without judgement.\n\n1. Run the safety check described in the rules.\n2. Summarise the period in a few lines: good days and bad days (counted if they logged them), flares, readings in the person's units exactly as given, appointments and any changes the care team made.\n3. What went well: routines that held, things that helped, small wins.\n4. What was hard: missed routines, triggers they noticed, practical or emotional obstacles, without blame.\n5. Patterns worth noting: only what the notes support, with counts and a caution about small numbers.\n6. Ask one or two questions to fill important gaps.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Safety check, The last few weeks, What went well, What was hard, Patterns worth noting, Questions for you.\n\nStop and wait for the person to correct or add to the review."},{"id":"adjust","file":"steps/02-adjust.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Small changes\n\nAgree one or two small changes for the coming weeks, inside the care plan.\n\n1. Ask what the person most wants to be different, and pick at most two areas from the review.\n2. For each, write an action plan: what exactly, when, how often, and what could get in the way, with a fallback for bad days. Ask how confident they are from 0 to 10; if below 7, make the step smaller.\n3. Keep changes to self-management: routines and reminders for medicines as prescribed, pacing and rest, tracking, household or work adjustments, social contact, sleep habits, coping strategies, and asking for help.\n4. Anything that would mean changing treatment goes to a \"for the care team\" list instead.\n5. Say what to track to know whether the change helped.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Focus, Action plan (table: What | When | Fallback | Confidence), What to track, For the care team.\n\nStop and wait for approval."},{"id":"prepare","file":"steps/03-prepare.md","stage":"plan","gate":"none","text":"# Step 3: Ready for the care team\n\nPrepare what the person needs for their next contact with the care team.\n\n1. A short summary they can read out or send: the period covered, key facts and readings exactly as given, flares, what they have tried, and their main concern.\n2. Questions, top three first: everything on the \"for the care team\" list, framed neutrally (\"Is there anything we should change about...?\"), plus anything unresolved from the review.\n3. When to contact the team before the next planned appointment, based on their plan, and how (advice line, portal, nurse).\n4. The date for the next check-in.\n\nWrite it as Markdown with sections Summary for your care team, Your questions, Do not wait if, Next check-in. Keep it to one page."}]}
